import{_ as e,o as a,c as o,ag as n}from"./chunks/framework.8tqBsFWt.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"Project Maturity","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"reference/concepts/project-maturity.md","filePath":"reference/concepts/project-maturity.md"}'),r={name:"reference/concepts/project-maturity.md"};function s(d,t,i,h,l,p){return a(),o("div",null,[...t[0]||(t[0]=[n('<h1 id="project-maturity" tabindex="-1">Project Maturity <a class="header-anchor" href="#project-maturity" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Project Maturity&quot;">​</a></h1><p>VERA judges a project against a ladder. Each step is a gate: it cannot be assessed at one step until every step below it is satisfied.</p><p>The ladder is ordered by <strong>how much VERA can truthfully say</strong> about a project — not by what to do next, which is a separate question the advice on each project page answers.</p><h2 id="the-ladder" tabindex="-1">The ladder <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-ladder" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The ladder&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>Name</th><th>Reached when</th><th>What it makes answerable</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0</td><td>Nominal</td><td>the project exists</td><td>nothing</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Owned</td><td>it has a project manager</td><td>accountability, and it appears in manager comparisons</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Scoped</td><td>it has a deadline and an hours budget, and a contract value if it is fixed price</td><td>&quot;late&quot; and &quot;over&quot; become meaningful; the burn-up, budget health and revenue all have something to measure against</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Staffed</td><td>at least one person has an allocation</td><td>committed capacity, and the allocation reading on the page</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Timed</td><td>every allocation says when its hours sit</td><td>capacity is spread across the weeks the work is actually expected in, rather than the whole life of the project</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Shaped</td><td>allocations carry a week-by-week plan</td><td>pace is judged against the plan the manager drew, rather than an even spread nobody agreed to</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Tracking</td><td>it is on course, within budget health, and will not run out of hours before its deadline</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Profitable</td><td>it is earning at or above the target ratio, with healthy margin and billing in step</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Closed well</td><td>it finished on time and on budget, and the plan it was measured against turned out to be right</td><td>the retrospective verdict</td></tr></tbody></table><p>A project&#39;s step is the highest one for which every step below it is also satisfied. One allocation missing its window drops an otherwise fully planned project back to <strong>Staffed</strong> — deliberately, so the advice points at the window rather than at the shape beyond it.</p><h2 id="recorded-time-is-a-gate-not-a-step" tabindex="-1">Recorded time is a gate, not a step <a class="header-anchor" href="#recorded-time-is-a-gate-not-a-step" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Recorded time is a gate, not a step&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Time is recorded from the first day, often before anyone has been allocated, so it is not a step of its own. It is a precondition on steps 6 and above: without recorded time those are <strong>unanswerable</strong>, which is not the same as failing. A brand-new project is not behind schedule; nothing is yet known about it.</p><h2 id="what-step-6-says-and-what-it-will-not-say" tabindex="-1">What step 6 says, and what it will not say <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-step-6-says-and-what-it-will-not-say" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What step 6 says, and what it will not say&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Once a project is planned and time is being recorded against it, VERA can tell whether it is tracking. It reports on that in a deliberately narrow way.</p><p><strong>When nothing at all has been recorded.</strong> If someone has an allocation here, weeks that have already finished expected hours from them, and nothing whatsoever has been recorded against the project, VERA names that person and says so. That is a gap in what VERA knows, not a statement about the person: the work may have been done and not logged. The wording says exactly that, and it is only ever shown to the people who run the project — never to the person it names.</p><p><strong>When time is recorded but differs from the plan.</strong> VERA says the project as a whole is above or below its plan, and points at the per-person view. It does not name anybody, because a plan that was not followed is as often a plan that was wrong.</p><p><strong>Never a ranking.</strong> VERA does not say who is furthest behind, does not order people by how far off they are, and uses no superlatives about anybody.</p><p><strong>Only finished weeks count.</strong> The week in progress is never judged, which is also why this does not change from one day to the next — it changes when a week closes.</p><p><strong>Pace is only raised when it is serious.</strong> The project page already reports schedule slippage as soon as it passes ten percent. Advice waits until twice that, so it stays quieter than the reading it sits above rather than repeating it.</p><h2 id="not-every-project-should-reach-the-top" tabindex="-1">Not every project should reach the top <a class="header-anchor" href="#not-every-project-should-reach-the-top" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Not every project should reach the top&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A project&#39;s kind sets how far up the ladder it can meaningfully climb, and stopping at that point means it is configured correctly — not that something is missing.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Kind</th><th>Stops at</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Internal, and all time-off projects</td><td>Owned</td><td>they carry no budget, deadline or allocations by design</td></tr><tr><td>Pro bono</td><td>Tracking</td><td>they earn nothing by definition, so profitability does not apply</td></tr><tr><td>Time and materials, fixed price</td><td>Closed well</td><td>the full ladder applies</td></tr><tr><td>Retainer and agile engagements</td><td>Owned</td><td>the engagement itself holds no period values; each period is assessed in full on its own</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Without these ceilings the advice would nag forever about projects that are set up exactly as intended.</p><h2 id="keeping-this-current" tabindex="-1">Keeping this current <a class="header-anchor" href="#keeping-this-current" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Keeping this current&quot;">​</a></h2><p>This page is the contract. The advice on each project page cites the step each piece of advice belongs to, and a test checks that every cited step exists here. When VERA gains a new capability it either belongs to a step already on this ladder, or it extends the top — it never quietly reorders the middle.</p>',21)])])}const g=e(r,[["render",s]]);export{u as __pageData,g as default};
